    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the test suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe customerCode is computed in `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` (a step in the `POST /api/owners` OfficeFloor pipeline), as `<REGION>-<HASH8>`. I added de-duplication there, which is the right place because the step runs *before* `AssignOwnerMembershipNumber` (which derives from `customerCode`) and before `SaveOwner`, so the de-duplicated value flows into every downstream field, the persisted record, and the response.\n\nThe change to `AssignOwnerCustomerCode.java`:\n- Injects `OwnerRepository` (by type, per the function-injection convention).\n- After computing `base = region + \"-\" + hash8`, checks it against every other owner's `customerCode`. Since the new owner isn't persisted yet, `findAll()` yields only the others; I still guard by id-identity for safety.\n- If `base` is free, keeps it; otherwise appends `-<n>` starting at `n=2`, incrementing to the smallest `n \u2265 2` that isn't already taken.\n- Updated the class Javadoc to document the collision rule.\n\nTest result: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`. The acceptance test `coreCustomerCodesStayUnique` asserts distinct owners always receive distinct customerCodes, which now holds even in the collision case.\n\nOne note on the guarantee: this de-dup is computed inside the request transaction from a repository read, so it's safe against already-persisted collisions but not against two concurrent in-flight POSTs racing before either commits \u2014 matching the existing pattern in `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique`, which does the same read-then-decide against `findAll()`.",
